The song was first recorded by [[Richard Chamberlain]] and released as a single in 1963 as "They Long to Be Close to You" (without parentheses).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://swedishcharts.com/showitem.asp?interpret=Richard+Chamberlain&titel=They+Long+To+Be+Close+To+You&cat=s |title=Richard Chamberlain – They Long To Be Close To You (Song) |year=1963 |publisher=[[Wikipedia:Record charts#Recommended charts|Hung Medien]] |access-date=April 27, 2019 }}
</ref> However, while the single's other [[A-side and B-side|side]], "[[Blue Guitar]]", became a hit, "They Long to Be Close to You" did not.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/richard-chamberlain/chart-history/hsi/ |title=Hot 100 - Billboard |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |year=1963 |access-date=April 27, 2019 }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/richard-chamberlain/chart-history/asi/ |title=Adult Contemporary Chart - Billboard |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |year=1963 |access-date=April 27, 2019 }}</ref> The tune was also recorded as a demo by [[Dionne Warwick]] in 1963, was re-recorded with a [[Burt Bacharach]] arrangement for her album ''[[Make Way for Dionne Warwick]]'' (1964), and was released as the B-side of her 1965 single "Here I Am". [[Dusty Springfield]] recorded the song in August 1964, but her version was not released commercially until it appeared on her album ''[[Where Am I Going?]]'' (1967). 

In 1970, [[Johnny Mathis]] recorded a version for his album ''[[Close to You (Johnny Mathis album)|Close to You]]''.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Album reviews|url=https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Record-World/70s/70/RW-1970-09-19-A.pdf|magazine=Record World|date=September 19, 1970|access-date=August 4, 2025|via=World Radio History|page=10|volume=25|issue=1214}}</ref> Also that year, [[Andy Williams]] recorded the song for his album ''[[The Andy Williams Show (album)|The Andy Williams Show]]'', not related to [[The Andy Williams Show|his television variety show of the same name]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Ruhlmann|first=William|url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/the-andy-williams-show-mw0000860357|title=The Andy Williams Show|website=[[AllMusic]]|access-date=August 4, 2025}}</ref> Bacharach released his own version in 1971.{{citation needed|date=August 2025}} Bobby Womack also covered this song in 1971. "Monologue / (They Long To Be) Close To You' was released in 1971 on his Communication album. The monologue on the intro of this song is Bobby's expression of frustration with the music industry.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}

A cover of the song was recorded by [[the Cranberries]] and released on the 2002 expended edition of the 1994 album ''[[No Need to Argue]]''.<ref>{{cite web |title=No Need To Argue (The Complete Sessions 1994-1995) |url=https://dn720702.ca.archive.org/0/items/cd_no-need-to-argue-the-complete-sessions-199_the-cranberries/cd_no-need-to-argue-the-complete-sessions-199_the-cranberries.pdf |website=Archive.org |access-date=3 June 2026}}</ref>

In 2024, [[Lady Gaga]] included her rendition of the song on ''[[Harlequin (Lady Gaga album)|Harlequin]]'', a companion album to the film ''[[Joker: Folie à Deux]]'', released on September 27, 2024.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Green |first1=Thomas H. |title=Album: Lady Gaga - Harlequin |url=https://theartsdesk.com/new-music/album-lady-gaga-harlequin |work=[[The Arts Desk]] |access-date=December 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251217035947/https://theartsdesk.com/new-music/album-lady-gaga-harlequin |archive-date=December 17, 2025 |date=September 28, 2024}}</ref>

In 2025, [[Kanye West]] sampled [[Stevie Wonder]]'s talkbox rendition of "Close to You" for his song "[[White Lines (Kanye West song)|White Lines]]" on his album ''[[Bully (album)|Bully]]''. The released version of "White Lines" in 2026 instead features [[André Troutman]] performing a talkbox interpolation of the song.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2025/music/news/kanye-west-album-bully-possibly-uncleared-samples-1236342896/|title=Kanye West's 'Bully' Is Loaded With (Possibly Uncleared) Samples and Covers: 'Close to You,' 'Can't Hurry Love,' Apparently a Verse From Playboi Carti, More|magazine=Variety|first=Jem|last=Aswad|date=March 19, 2026|access-date=April 12, 2026}}</ref>
